      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To develop a standardized, reliable, valid spine model of active trunk movements that accurately discriminates kinematic patterns of patients with chronic non-specific low back pain from those of healthy subjects."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Comparative cohort study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SUBJECTS",
          "content": "Healthy subjects (n = 25) and patients with chronic non-specific low back pain (n = 25) aged 30-65 years."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Subjects performed 7 trunk movements from a seated position at non-imposed speed during 2 sessions. Nine markers on bony landmarks measured range of motion and speed of 5 spinal segments, recorded by 8 optoelectronic cameras."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Both groups showed good-excellent reliability in all movements for range of motion and speed of all spinal segments (intraclass correlation (ICC), 0.70-0.96; standard error of measurement, expressed as a percentage, 19.4-3.3%). The minimal detectable change in the patient group was 16.7-53.7%. Range of motion and speed in all spinal segments for trunk flexion, rotation, and flexion with rotation differed significantly between groups (p < 0.001), with large/very large effect sizes (Cohen's d = 1.2-2). Binary logistic regression yielded sensitivities/specificities of 92%/84% for range of motion and 92%/80% for speed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Kinematic variables are valid, reliable measures and can be used clinically to diagnose chronic non-specific low back pain, manage treatment, and as quantitative outcome measures for clinical trial interventions."
        }
      ]},
